# Abstract {#h0003 kind=heading level=1}

::: paragraph {#p0012 kind=paragraph style="Normal"}
Smart agriculture represents a significant direction in modern agricultural development, with the rapid advancement of artificial intelligence technologies providing core technical support for the intelligent transformation of the entire agricultural production chain. This thesis conducts systematic research on the key applications of artificial intelligence in smart agriculture.
:::

::: paragraph {#p0013 kind=paragraph style="Normal"}
First, this study outlines the architecture and key technical foundations of smart agriculture, proposing a four-layer framework comprising the perception layer, transmission layer, platform layer, and application layer. It analyzes the technical characteristics of agricultural data collection and processing, and reviews the current applications of key AI methods including deep learning, time-series analysis, multimodal learning, and large language models in agriculture.
:::

::: paragraph {#p0014 kind=paragraph style="Normal"}
Second, in the area of crop growth monitoring and yield prediction, a hybrid deep learning model integrating convolutional neural networks and long short-term memory networks is designed. The model extracts features from multispectral imagery, meteorological data, and soil data, incorporating temporal attention mechanisms to focus on critical growth stage information. Experimental results demonstrate that the multi-source data fusion hybrid model significantly outperforms single-source models, achieving R2 values of 0.87 and 0.84 for rice and winter wheat yield prediction, respectively.
:::

::: paragraph {#p0015 kind=paragraph style="Normal"}
Third, in the area of agricultural pest and disease intelligent recognition and early warning, a lightweight detection method based on improved YOLOv8 is developed. Through GSConv modules, adaptive feature fusion, and decoupled head structure, the model substantially reduces parameter count while maintaining accuracy, achieving 91.3% mAP@0.5 with only 62% of the original model parameters. An integrated early warning system combining environmental monitoring and image recognition is also designed.
:::

::: paragraph {#p0016 kind=paragraph style="Normal"}
Finally, in applying large language models to agricultural technology services, an agricultural knowledge graph covering crops, pests, pesticides, fertilizers, and farming operations is constructed, containing over 80,000 entities and 500,000 relations. An intelligent agricultural Q&A system developed based on the Retrieval-Augmented Generation architecture achieved 86.7% expert approval accuracy on 150 test questions.
:::

::: paragraph {#p0017 kind=paragraph style="Normal"}
The research demonstrates that AI technologies can effectively improve agricultural production efficiency, reduce resource consumption, and enhance scientific decision-making. The thesis concludes by summarizing current challenges including data quality, technology costs, and talent shortages, and provides future perspectives on multimodal large models, edge computing, federated learning, and digital twins.
:::

